= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <vector> in the <nonnegative orthant>, the PDF's <matrix> gives the <quadratic form>
$$
x^TAx=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2\geq0.
$$
Thus it is a <copositive matrix>. On the other hand, with $u=(1,-1)^T$,
$$
\boxed{u^TAu=-2<0},
$$
so it is not a <positive semidefinite matrix>. Equivalently, the <eigenvalues> are $3$ and $-1$, with <eigenvectors> $(1,1)^T$ and $(1,-1)^T$.

**The local TeX has corrupted the <matrix> entries**. The PDF has diagonal entries one and off-diagonal entries two. The all-twos <matrix> in the TeX would instead have <quadratic form> $2(x_1+x_2)^2$ and would be a <positive semidefinite matrix>; it cannot demonstrate the requested distinction.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $x$ in the <nonnegative orthant>, the <positive semidefinite matrix> $P$ satisfies $x^TPx\geq0$. The <nonnegative matrix> $N$ satisfies
$$
x^TNx=\sum_{i,j}N_{ij}x_ix_j\geq0,
$$
since every summand is nonnegative. Adding these inequalities proves
$$
\boxed{x^T(P+N)x\geq0\quad(x\geq0)}.
$$
Consequently $A=P+N$ is a <copositive matrix>. The symmetry of $N$ follows from that of $A$ and $P$; entrywise nonnegativity alone need not imply <positive semidefiniteness>.

This gives the inclusion of the <positive-semidefinite-plus-nonnegative cone> in the <copositive cone>. It is only a sufficient construction; the argument does not claim that every <copositive matrix> has this decomposition in arbitrary <dimension>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For each fixed $x$ in the <nonnegative orthant>, the map $A\mapsto x^TAx$ is a continuous <linear function> on the <vector space> of real <symmetric matrices>. Thus
$$
K=\bigcap_{x\geq0}\{A:\langle A,xx^T\rangle_F\geq0\}
$$
is an intersection of <closed half-spaces>, using the <Frobenius inner product> $\langle A,B\rangle_F=\operatorname{tr}(AB)$ on this space. Arbitrary intersections of <closed sets> are closed, so $K$ is closed.

If $A,B\in K$ and $s,t\geq0$, then $x^T(sA+tB)x=sx^TAx+tx^TBx\geq0$ for every $x\geq0$. Therefore $sA+tB\in K$, including the zero <matrix> when $s=t=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{K\text{ is a closed convex cone}}.
$$
The <copositive cone> is defined in the real space of <symmetric matrices>; symmetry is needed later to recover every <matrix> entry from these <quadratic forms>.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

A <pointed cone> has no nonzero line through the origin: equivalently $K\cap(-K)=\{0\}$. If $A\in K\cap(-K)$, its <quadratic form> vanishes on the <nonnegative orthant>. Testing the coordinate <vectors> gives $A_{ii}=0$. Testing $e_i+e_j$ then gives
$$
0=(e_i+e_j)^TA(e_i+e_j)=2A_{ij}.
$$
Since $A$ is a <symmetric matrix>, every entry is zero. Thus $\boxed{K\cap(-K)=\{0\}}$.

The <identity matrix> gives an <interior> point. For a symmetric perturbation $E$ with <operator norm> $\|E\|_2<1$, the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives
$$
x^T(I+E)x\geq(1-\|E\|_2)\|x\|_2^2>0\quad(x\ne0).
$$
This open <norm> ball around $I$ lies even in the <positive semidefinite cone>, hence in $K$. Consequently $\boxed{I\in\operatorname{int}K}$ and the <interior> is nonempty.

More generally, the <interior of the copositive cone> consists exactly of <strictly copositive matrices>. Positivity on the <compact> nonnegative <unit sphere> has a positive minimum and persists under small perturbations; a zero there is destroyed by an arbitrarily small negative multiple of $I$.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For a <convex cone> $D$ in an <inner product> space, use the nonnegative-pairing convention
$$
D^*=\{B:\langle A,B\rangle\geq0\text{ for every }A\in D\}.
$$
Here the pairing is the <Frobenius inner product> on real <symmetric matrices>. Let $C_0$ be the <conic hull> of the nonnegative <rank-one matrices> $xx^T$, and let $C=\overline{C_0}$. For $A\in K$, $\langle A,xx^T\rangle_F=x^TAx\geq0$. This extends to <conic combinations>, and by <continuity> to their limits. Hence $C\subseteq K^*$.

For the converse, if $B\notin C$, <separation from a closed convex cone> supplies a symmetric $H$ with
$$
\langle H,B\rangle_F<0,\qquad
\langle H,Z\rangle_F\geq0\quad(Z\in C).
$$
In particular $x^THx\geq0$ for every $x\geq0$, so $H\in K$. The negative pairing then excludes $B$ from $K^*$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{K^*=\overline{\operatorname{cone}\{xx^T:x\geq0\}}}.
$$
The same generator test gives $C^*=K$. This is the <duality of copositive and completely positive cones>; the next argument removes the closure.

= f
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The real <vector space> of <symmetric matrices> has <dimension> $N=n(n+1)/2$. By the <conic Carathéodory theorem>, every member of $C_0$ is a <conic combination> of at most $N$ generators. Absorb each nonnegative <coefficient> into its <vector> through $\lambda xx^T=(\sqrt\lambda x)(\sqrt\lambda x)^T$.

If $B_\ell\in C_0$ converges to $B$, write, padding with zero <vectors> if necessary,
$$
B_\ell=\sum_{j=1}^N x_{\ell j}x_{\ell j}^T,\qquad x_{\ell j}\geq0.
$$
The <matrix trace> satisfies
$$
\operatorname{tr}B_\ell=\sum_{j=1}^N\|x_{\ell j}\|_2^2.
$$
The left side is bounded because $B_\ell$ converges. Thus the finite tuple $(x_{\ell1},\ldots,x_{\ell N})$ is bounded. The <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> gives a subsequence on which every <vector> converges, say $x_{\ell j}\to x_j\geq0$. <Continuity> of the <outer product> now gives $B=\sum_{j=1}^N x_jx_j^T\in C_0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{C_0\text{ is closed},\qquad K^*=C=C_0}.
$$
This proves <closedness of the completely positive cone>. The uniform bound on the number of factors and the <matrix trace> bound are both essential: an arbitrary <conic hull> of a closed generating set need not be closed.

= g
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

Let $U=\{x\geq0:\|x\|_2=1\}$. This set is nonempty and <compact> for $n\geq1$, and the <quadratic form> is continuous. Its minimum $\alpha$ is therefore attained.

For every nonzero $y$ in the <nonnegative orthant>, normalizing $y$ gives
$$
y^T(Q-\lambda I)y
=\|y\|_2^2\left[\left(\frac y{\|y\|_2}\right)^T
Q\left(\frac y{\|y\|_2}\right)-\lambda\right].
$$
The zero <vector> imposes no further condition. It follows that $Q-\lambda I$ is a <copositive matrix> exactly when $\lambda\leq\alpha$. Thus the feasible <scalar> set is $(-\infty,\alpha]$, and
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\max\{\lambda:Q-\lambda I\in K\}}.
$$
Both optima are attained. This <copositive reformulation of an orthant Rayleigh minimum> follows directly from homogeneity; it does not require invoking a duality theorem for the original nonconvex constraint.

The restriction to $x\geq0$ matters. For the <matrix> in part (a), this minimum is $1$ whereas the unrestricted smallest <eigenvalue> is $-1$.

= h
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=h}

For $X$ in the <dual cone> $C$, copositivity of $Q-\lambda I$ gives $\langle X,Q-\lambda I\rangle_F\geq0$. An upper-bounding <Lagrangian> for the maximization is therefore
$$
L(\lambda,X)=\lambda+\langle X,Q-\lambda I\rangle_F
=\langle Q,X\rangle_F+\lambda(1-\operatorname{tr}X).
$$
Its <supremum> over unrestricted $\lambda$ is finite precisely when $\operatorname{tr}X=1$. Minimizing that upper bound gives the <conic program>
$$
\boxed{\min_{X\in C}\ \langle Q,X\rangle_F
\quad\text{subject to }\operatorname{tr}X=1}.
$$
This is <completely positive optimization>, not simply <semidefinite programming>: $C$ is the <completely positive cone>.

There is also a direct equality certificate. Every feasible $X=\sum_j u_ju_j^T$, $u_j\geq0$, has $\sum_j\|u_j\|_2^2=1$. Its objective is a weighted average of the nonnegative-sphere <Rayleigh quotients>, hence is at least $\alpha$. For a minimizing unit <vector> $x_*$ from part (g), $X_*=x_*x_*^T$ is feasible and has objective $\alpha$. Thus $\boxed{X_*=x_*x_*^T}$ attains the dual and both values agree, without relying on unverified regularity assumptions.

